::: {.callout-exercise #ex-public_health .core}

### Reading UK public health monitoring data

[**Core**]{.core-text}

The UK government provides some public health data for England (other UK countries have data available elsewhere) on their data portal, here - https://ukhsa-dashboard.data.gov.uk/. The data is available for download in CSV format.

Have a look through the datasets with measurements taken over time which are available on this page. Choose a category, then select a dataset that is displayed as a line plot with a **single line**. Download the CSV formatted data to your computer. You can find these by clicking on the graph then choosing "Download", then "CSV format". Move this file into the `{{< meta practical >}}/data` folder of your `{{< meta git_repo >}}`  folder

::: {.callout-note}
If you have difficulty downloading a dataset, use the example rhinovirus dataset provided in the data folder as `data/ukhsa-chart-download-rhinovirus.csv`.
:::

In a new Quarto report, `{{< meta practical >}}/ukhsa_data_analysis.qmd`, load this table into R and then view the resulting table with `gt()`. These tables are mostly long, so we can use the `head()` function together with `gt()` to just visualise the first ten lines:

For example:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
rhinovirus <- read_csv("data/ukhsa-chart-download-rhinovirus.csv")

gt(head(rhinovirus))
```

